- How would you adapt for deletions from the stream?
- What changes if k can grow or shrink over time?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of DAG longest-path DP and cycle detection using topological sort, a common scheduling/build scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sort (Kahn's algorithm)
- Longest path in a DAG via DP
- Cycle detection via processed-node count
- Handling multiple prerequisites and parallel tasks

## Code Example
```javascript
function computeEarliestFinish(n, durations, edges) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const est = durations.slice();
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let processed = 0;
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    processed++;
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      if (est[v] < est[u] + durations[v]) est[v] = est[u] + durations[v];
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  if (processed !== n) throw new Error('Cycle detected');
  return { est, total: Math.max(...est) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the actual critical path from est and predecessors?
- How modify for weighted edges representing inter-task setup times instead of node durations?
- How to scale and detect cycles efficiently in streaming graphs?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses interval DP, proper subproblem decomposition, and practical optimization with prefix sums. Builds intuition for product costs over contiguous blocks.

## Key Concepts
- Interval DP on [i, j]
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Bottom-up filling by increasing length; base case 0 for single element

## Code Example
```javascript
function minMergeCost(stones){
  const n = stones.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + stones[i];
  const sum = (i,j) => pref[j+1]-pref[i];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let len=2; len<=n; len++){
    for(let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++){
      const j = i+len-1;
      let best = Infinity;
      for(let k=i; k<j; k++){
        const cost = dp[i][k] + dp[k+1][j] + sum(i,j);
        if(cost < best) best = cost;
      }
      dp[i][j] = best;
    }
  }
  return n>0 ? dp[0][n-1] : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for K-ary merging (K > 2)?
- What optimizations apply if n is large and many merges share sums?

## Why This Is Asked
This probes understanding of iterative approaches, partition logic, and complexity trade-offs between quicksort and mergesort. 

## Key Concepts
- Iterative quicksort with explicit stack
- Hoare partition scheme and pivot strategy
- Time/space complexity comparisons with mergesort

## Code Example
```javascript
function iterativeQuicksort(a){
  const n=a.length;
  const stack=[[0,n-1]];
  const swap=(i,j)=>{let t=a[i]; a[i]=a[j]; a[j]=t;};
  while(stack.length){
    const [l,r]=stack.pop();
    if(l>=r) continue;
    let pivot=a[(l+r)>>1];
    let i=l, j=r;
    while(i<=j){
      while(a[i]<pivot) i++;
      while(a[j]>pivot) j--;
      if(i<=j){ swap(i,j); i++; j--; }
    }
    if(l<j) stack.push([l,j]);
    if(i<r) stack.push([i,r]);
  }
  return a;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you modify to guarantee O(n log n) worst-case?
- Compare stability with mergesort; when would you choose one over the other?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of stability and permutation-based sorting, ensuring candidates can separate data from its keys and reason about time/space trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Stable sort
- Permutations
- Time/space complexity
- Merge-based sorting on indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function stableSortIndicesByValue(A){
  const idx = Array.from({length: A.length}, (_, i) => i);
  function merge(left, right){
    const res = [];
    let i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < left.length && j < right.length){
      if (A[left[i]] <= A[right[j]]) res.push(left[i++]);
      else res.push(right[j++]);
    }
    while (i < left.length) res.push(left[i++]);
    while (j < right.length) res.push(right[j++]);
    return res;
  }
  function sort(arr){
    if (arr.length <= 1) return arr;
    const m = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
    const L = sort(arr.slice(0, m));
    const R = sort(arr.slice(m));
    return merge(L, R);
  }
  const P = sort(idx);
  return { P, sorted: P.map(i => A[i]) };
}
```

## Concept Overview
An LRU (Least Recently Used) cache efficiently manages memory by evicting the least accessed items when capacity is reached. This implementation leverages a HashMap for fast key lookups and a DoublyLinkedList for maintaining access order.

## Implementation Details
- **HashMap**: Maps keys to Node references, enabling O(1) lookup operations
- **DoublyLinkedList**: Maintains access order with head representing most recently used items and tail representing least recently used items
- **Thread Safety**: ReentrantReadWriteLock provides optimal concurrency - multiple threads can read simultaneously while writes are mutually exclusive
- **Eviction Policy**: When capacity is exceeded, the tail node (least recently used) is removed
- **Operations**: Both get() and put() operations maintain O(1) time complexity

## Code Example
```java
public class LRUCache<K, V> {
    private final Map<K, Node<K, V>> cache;
    private final DoublyLinkedList<K, V> order;
    private final ReentrantReadWriteLock lock;
    private final int capacity;

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.cache = new HashMap<>();
        this.order = new DoublyLinkedList<>();
        this.lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        lock.readLock().lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> node = cache.get(key);
            if (node != null) {
                // Upgrade to write lock for reordering
                lock.readLock().unlock();
                lock.writeLock().lock();
                order.moveToHead(node);
                return node.value;
            }
            return null;
        } finally {
            if (lock.isWriteLocked()) {
                lock.writeLock().unlock();
            } else {
                lock.readLock().unlock();
            }
        }
    }

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> existing = cache.get(key);
            if (existing != null) {
                existing.value = value;
                order.moveToHead(existing);
                return;
            }

            Node<K, V> newNode = new Node<>(key, value);
            cache.put(key, newNode);
            order.addToHead(newNode);

            if (cache.size() > capacity) {
                Node<K, V> tail = order.removeTail();
                cache.remove(tail.key);
            }
        } finally {
            lock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
    }
}```

## Why This Is Asked
Beginner DP concepts with a new angle: counting unordered combinations (coin change ways) and contrasting memoization with tabulation.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming (top-down memoization)
- Tabulation (bottom-up DP)
- Unordered combinations vs permutations
- Space optimization (1D DP)

## Code Example
```javascript
function countWaysTopDown(coins, target) {
  const n = coins.length;
  const memo = new Map();
  function dfs(i, rem) {
    if (rem === 0) return 1;
    if (rem < 0 || i === n) return 0;
    const key = i + '|' + rem;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const res = dfs(i + 1, rem) + dfs(i, rem - coins[i]);
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0, target);
}

function countWaysBottomUp(coins, target) {
  const dp = new Array(target + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const c of coins) {
    for (let r = c; r <= target; r++) {
      dp[r] += dp[r - c];
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you count permutations (order matters) instead of combinations?
- How would you handle very large targets or mod results to avoid overflow in languages with fixed integer sizes?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses augmenting a Trie to answer prefix-based queries with dynamic weights while maintaining efficient updates. Encourages careful handling of edge cases and update semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Trie augmentation with maxSubtreeWeight; non-decreasing weight updates.
- O(len(word)) insert and O(len(s)) query.
- Edge cases: empty word input, overlapping prefixes, no matches.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.wordWeight = 0;
    this.hasWord = false;
    this.maxSubtreeWeight = 0;
  }
}
class PrefixWeightTrie {
  constructor() { this.root = new Node(); }
  insert(word, weight) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new Node());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    if (!node.hasWord || weight > node.wordWeight) {
      node.hasWord = true;
      node.wordWeight = weight;
    }
    // update maxSubtreeWeight along path (omitted detailed propagation for brevity)
  }
  queryLongestPrefix(s) {
    let node = this.root;
    let bestLen = 0, bestWeight = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
      const c = s[i];
      if (!node.next.has(c)) break;
      node = node.next.get(c);
      if (node.maxSubtreeWeight > 0) {
        bestLen = i + 1;
        bestWeight = node.maxSubtreeWeight;
      }
    }
    return [bestLen, bestWeight];
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP state design, memoization vs tabulation, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions; state i is start index; transitions extend end while sum <= limit.
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums; reconstruction via choice pointers.
- Edge cases: impossible when an element exceeds limit; handling ties.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minPartitionCost(nums, limit){
  const n = nums.length, pref=[0];
  for(const x of nums) pref.push(pref[pref.length-1]+x);
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(null);
  function dfs(i){
    if(i===n) return [0,[]];
    if(memo[i]) return memo[i];
    let best=[Infinity, null];
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){ const sum=pref[j+1]-pref[i]; if(sum>limit) break; const sub=dfs(j+1); const cost=(j-i+1)**2+sub[0]; if(cost<best[0]) best=[cost, [i,j].concat(sub[1]||[])]; }
    memo[i]=best; return best;
  }
  return dfs(0);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you modify to also return the actual blocks boundaries when multiple optimal partitions exist?
- How does the solution change if limit is dynamic per block?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to fuse graph shortest-path ideas with node activation delays and cycle handling, plus choosing between DAG DP and general graph Dijkstra.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra with transformed edge costs w(u,v)+d(v)
- Topological sort for DAG optimization
- Handling multiple sources S
- Non-negative weights guarantee correctness

## Code Example
```javascript
function earliestFinish(V, adj, S, d) {
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = new Array(V).fill(INF);
  const pq = new MinHeap((a,b)=>a[0]-b[0]);
  for (const s of S) { dist[s] = d[s]; pq.push([dist[s], s]); }
  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
    const [du, u] = pq.pop();
    if (du !== dist[u]) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      const cand = dist[u] + w + d[v];
      if (cand < dist[v]) { dist[v] = cand; pq.push([cand, v]); }
    }
  }
  return dist;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses your ability to design a compact prefix-search data structure and implement tie-breaking logic using a Trie.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with terminal frequencies stored at each node
- Prefix search via traversal to the prefix end node
- DFS collection of all words in the subtree
- Sorting by frequency (descending), then lexicographic order for ties

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.freq = 0;
  }
}

class Autocomplete {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  addWord(word, freq) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    node.freq = freq;
  }

  getSuggestions(prefix, limit) {
    if (limit <= 0) return [];

    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }

    const results = [];
    const dfs = (currentNode, path) => {
      if (currentNode.freq > 0) {
        results.push({ word: path, freq: currentNode.freq });
      }
      for (const [char, childNode] of currentNode.children) {
        dfs(childNode, path + char);
      }
    };

    dfs(node, prefix);

    results.sort((a, b) => {
      if (b.freq !== a.freq) return b.freq - a.freq;
      return a.word.localeCompare(b.word);
    });

    return results.slice(0, limit).map(r => r.word);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical Trie usage for autocomplete, encouraging explicit design choices and complexity reasoning.

## Key Concepts

- Trie node with 26-child array and isWord flag
- DFS enumeration in lexicographic order
- Edge cases: empty string, duplicates, limit handling, memory usage

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() { this.next = new Array(26).fill(null); this.isWord = false; }
}
class Trie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(w) { let p = this.root; for (const ch of w) { const i = ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if (!p.next[i]) p.next[i] = new TrieNode(); p = p.next[i]; } p.isWord = true; }
  search(w) { let p = this.root; for (const ch of w) { const i = ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if (!p.next[i]) return false; p = p.next[i]; } return p.isWord; }
  startsWith(pfx) { let p = this.root; for (const ch of pfx) { const i = ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if (!p.next[i]) return false; p = p.next[i]; } return true; }
  listWithPrefix(pfx, limit) { const res = []; let p = this.root; for (const ch of pfx) { const i = ch.charCodeAt(0)-97; if (!p.next[i]) return res; p = p.next[i]; } const dfs = (node, prefix) => { if (res.length >= limit) return; if (node.isWord) res.push(prefix); for (let c = 0; c < 26; c++) { const child = node.next[c]; if (child) dfs(child, prefix + String.fromCharCode(97+c)); } }; dfs(p, pfx); return res; }
}
```

